So

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.720
because this is a public hearing for the record, my name is John Viscowski, I'm the town's planner on a consulting basis.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.240
I

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.560
have been working with the

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.320
Andover Planning and Zoning Commission, which is the formal applicant on this

00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:44.105
to incorporate a number of changes into their

00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:45.705
zoning regulations,

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.545
to reflect requirements

00:00:47.785 --> 00:00:51.865
that were included as part of Public Act 20 five-one,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.320
which

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.280
was approved in special session last November

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.440
by the Connecticut General Assembly.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.800
It was a large bill and it included several

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.040
changes that every municipality in the state needed to make relative to their zoning regulations

00:01:07.735 --> 00:01:09.655
that were incorporated as part of

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:11.175
amendments to

00:01:12.055 --> 00:01:14.295
Connecticut General Statute Section eight-two,

00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:16.055
which is the zoning enabling statute.

00:01:16.295 --> 00:01:18.615
I'll ask Mr. Chair, can you see my screen?

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.680
Yes, So thank

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.200
>> this, what I'm gonna be walking through is just an excerpt

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.560
of the specific sections of the regulations, which will be

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.400
proposed for amendment

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.120
pursuant to Public Act 20 five-one.

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:36.415
For

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:41.775
folks who were not here at the last couple of meetings, I've gone through this briefly with the commission

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.735
and I'll provide a brief overview.

00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:47.535
Again, Public Act 20 five-one

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.870
made a couple of changes

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.790
relative to parking

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.670
regulations and multifamily regulations,

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.150
again, that every municipality is required to make.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.310
The two general areas

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:00.510
concerned

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.030
what is called

00:02:03.345 --> 00:02:06.625
transit oriented middle housing or just middle housing,

00:02:06.785 --> 00:02:09.505
which is defined by the new law as

00:02:10.865 --> 00:02:11.585
a

00:02:12.625 --> 00:02:16.705
multifamily housing unit of between two and nine units.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.320
The new law required

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.880
that those be allowed

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:22.840
to

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.360
be constructed

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.040
in all commercial

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.880
and mixed

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.200
use zoning districts in every municipality in the state

00:02:32.205 --> 00:02:40.045
by only a, what they refer to as a summary review process, which we would consider essentially a site plan review process,

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:43.885
which is essentially an as of right administrative approval

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.410
put in place by the commission with no

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:54.690
special conditions and no public hearing process. So it's an as of right use in commercial and mixed use zones.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.730
Municipalities are still able to regulate

00:02:59.525 --> 00:03:03.685
the style and standards for that middle housing in those zones,

00:03:03.925 --> 00:03:06.565
but it must be an as of right use.

00:03:07.445 --> 00:03:09.845
That's change number one. Change number two

00:03:10.725 --> 00:03:12.565
is in regards to the

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.270
minimum number of off street

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:20.310
parking spaces that can be required for a residential development.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:25.030
The new law eliminates essentially

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:26.470
parking,

00:03:26.865 --> 00:03:33.105
minimum parking requirements for multifamily or residential developments of 16 units or under.

00:03:33.505 --> 00:03:35.745
It basically says the commission cannot

00:03:35.825 --> 00:03:36.705
deny

00:03:36.785 --> 00:03:41.570
a multifamily development or a residential development of 16 units or under for

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.650
failure to provide

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:45.970
off street parking.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:48.850
And it caps the minimum

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.370
off street parking numbers of

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.490
developments over 16 residential units

00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:58.295
to one parking space per

00:03:58.935 --> 00:04:01.735
studio or one bedroom and two parking spaces per

00:04:01.895 --> 00:04:02.535
three,

00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:04.375
two or more bedrooms.

00:04:05.175 --> 00:04:06.695
So again, it eliminates

00:04:07.575 --> 00:04:09.975
a commission's ability to require,

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.570
you know, in some cases, any parking at all,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.570
and in most cases, an excess of parking

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.330
that chews up,

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.450
you know, development sites and reduces

00:04:22.625 --> 00:04:28.545
allowable densities. In the case of Andover are many communities that rely almost exclusively on

00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:31.345
vehicles for transportation,

00:04:31.345 --> 00:04:35.905
which is to say we don't have buses, we don't have trains, we don't have Metro lines.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.950
This is really,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:46.710
you know, going to have very, very little impact because there is no reason that a developer who's building a multifamily

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:48.870
in a market situation

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.470
would not provide,

00:04:51.585 --> 00:04:54.625
you know, sufficient parking for his or her tenants.

00:04:55.345 --> 00:05:04.545
So, you know, the developer is still able and allowed to provide parking should they wish, it is just limits the ability of the commission to regulate

00:05:04.865 --> 00:05:06.545
the minimum numbers of that parking.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:14.490
So those are the two major changes. And so what we did to be responsive to those changes, so we added in

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.010
section four of the general regulations,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.890
a new section, section 4.23

00:05:21.145 --> 00:05:24.905
about middle housing in which we regulate what middle housing looks like.

00:05:25.945 --> 00:05:28.985
I'll go to that section now, let me make this a little bit bigger.

00:05:29.625 --> 00:05:32.425
So middle housing creates standards for this.

00:05:32.905 --> 00:05:35.225
And again, we'll define it later in the regulations.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.590
These are the standards that will apply.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.070
Again, the only place that this is required

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:43.350
would be

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.310
the business district, which is the town's only

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.685
landed commercial or mixed use district.

00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:54.005
The town has a mixed use floating district that already allows housing,

00:05:54.245 --> 00:05:57.285
but that again is not a landed district, it's a floating district.

00:05:57.365 --> 00:05:58.885
So this would not apply

00:05:59.285 --> 00:06:01.605
exclusively to that. So this would just be a business district.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.250
So basic minimum

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.890
structural setbacks that basically mirror

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:15.770
the structural setbacks for the housing or for the business district anyway, 50 foot front yard, 50 foot rear yard, 25 foot sides,

00:06:16.925 --> 00:06:21.485
a distance of 25 feet in between individual structures within the development,

00:06:22.125 --> 00:06:27.885
standard height requirement of maximum of two and a half stories at 35 feet, maximum impervious

00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:31.005
50%. And again, all of this basically tracks

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.110
commercial development standards.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.630
We did add a minimum landscape buffer

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:41.830
of evergreen plantings five feet wide and a minimum of five feet tall at time of planting,

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:43.430
or a

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:53.095
opaque fence, a minimum of four feet high in order to create an effective year round visual buffer between properties in order to buffer residential from non residential uses.

00:06:53.975 --> 00:06:57.255
We do have maximum building size and architectural

00:06:57.255 --> 00:07:02.740
design standards elsewhere in the regulations. So instead of coming up with new standards for

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:08.980
building sizes and architectural standards, we basically just referenced the other sections of the existing

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.340
regs.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:17.215
So that's, it's fairly basic, but again, our regulations tend to be fairly straightforward and

00:07:17.455 --> 00:07:18.175
mostly

00:07:20.255 --> 00:07:21.695
bulk and loading based

00:07:22.015 --> 00:07:26.175
for developments in the business district anyway. So this tends to reflect that.

