00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.960
closely tracking the state requirement.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:27.200
There is also as part of that parking standard,

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.160
if the town requires, let's say

00:17:30.525 --> 00:17:32.925
two parking spaces per, you know,

00:17:33.405 --> 00:17:44.125
two bedroom multifamily, there's a townhouse complex proposed multifamily complex of two bedroom units, say it's 16, let's say it's 20 units, we would require 40 spaces.

00:17:44.365 --> 00:17:48.830
If for some reason the developer believes they don't need that many,

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:53.710
this statute also provides the ability for a developer applicant

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.430
to submit a parking needs assessment,

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.310
which is defined in the statute,

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:00.270
which would basically

00:18:01.045 --> 00:18:04.485
be the developer's opportunity to convince the commission

00:18:04.725 --> 00:18:12.965
that because of some circumstances unique to their location and their development type, they don't need to provide even that bare minimum of parking.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:26.540
They can do the analysis. Let's basically say it's, you know, a senior residence housing right next to the center of the community center and a dial a ride bus stop,

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:31.865
and really only one spot per unit is going to be required,

00:18:32.265 --> 00:18:43.785
and they're so restricted in terms of their square footage that they want to reduce the number of parking. In a circumstance, something like that, they could make an argument that they need fewer parking spaces so they can do this analysis,

00:18:44.025 --> 00:18:46.665
and this provides the opportunity for them to do so.

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:54.080
Again, that's going to be fairly extreme cases, and again, in a community such as Andover,

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.680
I don't suspect we're going to see many, if any of those at all.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.160
But again, the regulations by the statute are required.

00:19:03.735 --> 00:19:07.175
Then we go into definitions. So we add a definition for

00:19:07.415 --> 00:19:15.175
conservation and traffic mitigation district. This is an area of town that if the town so desired, it could designate

00:19:15.495 --> 00:19:17.975
as a high parking demand area

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:25.330
in which the town could also provide regulations for those developments of under 16 units.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.770
So if there is an area of town that is so parking constricted,

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:30.370
and here I'm thinking

00:19:30.895 --> 00:19:41.135
a community like Mystic. If you're in Downtown Mystic, that's an extremely parking constrained area where even if you added four or six or eight residential units,

00:19:41.295 --> 00:19:44.415
you would create, you know, untenable parking demand.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.480
I don't necessarily think Andover has that, but the

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:55.720
town does have the ability to designate an area like that, and this provides a definition for that

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:58.760
where we could require

00:20:00.005 --> 00:20:00.885
off street parking.

00:20:01.365 --> 00:20:03.845
We did not have a definition of multifamily

00:20:04.085 --> 00:20:14.565
dwellings or single family dwellings, just to be clear, multiple one and two family dwellings, just adding definitions of to make sure that we're being clear about what we're referring to. So we're adding those.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:24.590
We are also adding definitions for middle housing, which is what we talked about, which is two through nine units. And again, this could be in a single

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:28.670
structure, you know, a duplex, it could be an

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:29.390
unit

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:37.235
apartment, it could be townhouses, it could be two quadplexes, it could be multiple buildings. So in whatever arrangement,

00:20:37.555 --> 00:20:40.115
two through nine units is referred to as middle

00:20:40.115 --> 00:20:40.755
housing.

00:20:41.315 --> 00:20:49.760
And then we define mixed use development, which we have a provision for in the regulations, but do not define it, so we want to ensure that we define it properly.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.120
And then we also add a definition for summary review,

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:59.875
which is the term of art in the statute that we are basically equating to

00:21:00.435 --> 00:21:03.475
site plan review, which is something that the commission does have.

00:21:04.675 --> 00:21:06.675
And this is something that would be allowable

00:21:07.315 --> 00:21:13.150
for middle housing specifically in commercial or mixed use districts, whereby the commission must

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:15.310
do essentially a site plan review,

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.030
no public hearing, no conditional approvals.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.590
And should this commission wish,

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:21.950
it could designate

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:23.550
summary review to

00:21:23.885 --> 00:21:31.005
its zoning enforcement officer, if you wished. Obviously, we have a longstanding practice of seeing these by the full commission,

00:21:31.325 --> 00:21:33.645
but just providing a provision for that.

00:21:34.525 --> 00:21:37.805
So those are the definitions that we're adding, and that is

00:21:38.045 --> 00:21:41.460
the conclusion of the proposed regulations

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.700
as required by the state statute.

